{
  "subject": {
    "slug": "radiation-biology",
    "title": "Radiation Biology",
    "order": 7
  },
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "radiation-effects",
    "title": "Radiation Effects: Total Body Response & Late Effects",
    "order": 0,
    "pages": [
      {
        "title": "Two Timeframes of Radiation Response",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Radiation effects are generally grouped into two timeframes: "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "early (acute)",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " changes appearing within about 6 months of exposure, and "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "late (chronic)",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " changes appearing more than 6 months later \u2014 often as a consequence of irreversible early changes that progress over time. This lesson covers acute whole-body response first, then late/stochastic effects."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Requirements for Acute Radiation Syndrome",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Acute Radiation Syndrome (ARS)",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " requires three specific exposure conditions to be met: (1) the exposure must be "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "acute",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (delivered within minutes), (2) it must involve "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "total-body or near-total-body",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " exposure, and (3) it must come from an "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "external, penetrating source",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " \u2014 not ingested, inhaled, or implanted radioactive material."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Human data on ARS comes from a genuinely limited set of sources: industrial and laboratory accidents, nuclear weapons testing fallout, therapeutic medical exposures, Hiroshima and Nagasaki, and the Chernobyl reactor accident."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The Three Phases of Every Syndrome",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Each of the three classic ARS syndromes moves through the same three phases, though timing and severity differ by syndrome and dose:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "orderedList",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Prodromal phase",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 symptoms (gastrointestinal, neurologic, or both) appear, correlating with dose"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Latent phase",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 the person appears symptom-free, even though damage is actively progressing internally"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Manifest illness phase",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 the effects of exposure become clinically evident, correlating with dose"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Hematopoietic Syndrome",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Induced by total-body doses of "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "100\u20131000 cGy",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": ". The human LD"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "50/60",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "subscript"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (dose lethal to 50% of exposed people within 60 days) is estimated between 350\u2013450 cGy, varying with age, health, and sex \u2014 females tend to be somewhat more resistant, and the very young and very old somewhat more sensitive."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Prodromal symptoms (nausea, vomiting) appear within hours. The latent phase lasts days to about 3 weeks \u2014 the person feels fine, but bone marrow stem cells are actively dying. The manifest illness phase (3\u20135 weeks post-exposure) brings "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "pancytopenia",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " (depression of all blood cell counts): anemia (fewer red cells), hemorrhage (fewer platelets), and serious infection (fewer white cells). Death, if it occurs, is primarily from infection and hemorrhage."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "GI and CNS Syndromes",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Gastrointestinal (GI) syndrome",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": ": induced by doses of roughly 1000\u201310,000 cGy (as low as 600 cGy in some cases). Above 1000 cGy, severe depopulation of intestinal crypt cells causes partial or complete denudation of the villi lining the small intestine \u2014 leading to decreased absorption, fluid leakage into the lumen (dehydration), and bacterial invasion into the bloodstream. Mean survival time is 3\u201310 days."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Cerebrovascular (CNS) syndrome",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": ": occurs at doses greater than 100 Gy (some sources note overlap with GI syndrome symptoms starting around 5000 cGy). Onset is within a few hours; death typically occurs within 24\u201372 hours. No known treatment exists once the syndrome develops \u2014 supportive/comfort care is the only option."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page5_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Which acute radiation syndrome has the shortest survival time and occurs at the highest total-body doses?"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "Hematopoietic syndrome",
              "Cerebrovascular (CNS) syndrome"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "CNS syndrome occurs at the highest doses (>100 Gy) and has by far the shortest survival time \u2014 hours to a few days."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Deterministic vs. Stochastic \u2014 A Quick Recap",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Everything covered so far (hematopoietic, GI, CNS syndromes) is "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "deterministic",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": ": produced by high doses, with severity increasing above a threshold. From here, this lesson shifts to "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "stochastic",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " effects \u2014 low-dose, long-term (chronic) exposure, where increasing dose increases "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "probability",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "italic"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " of an effect, not its severity, and no threshold is assumed."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Radiation-Induced Malignancy",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Much of what's known about radiation-induced cancer in humans comes from a few major population studies: Hiroshima/Nagasaki atomic bomb survivors, American radiologists (early 20th century), ankylosing spondylitis patients treated with therapeutic radiation, and radium watch-dial painters."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Radiation-induced leukemia",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " follows a linear, nonthreshold dose-response relationship, with a latent period of 4\u20137 years and an at-risk period of about 20 years. Bomb survivor data showed leukemia incidence up to 100 times that of the nonirradiated population at high doses."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Other cancers with well-documented radiation associations include "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "thyroid cancer",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (from childhood thymic irradiation and fallout exposure, ~20-year latent period), "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "bone cancer",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (radium watch-dial painters, who ingested radium via paintbrush-tipping habits), "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "skin cancer",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (a threshold effect, unlike most other radiation-induced cancers), and "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "breast cancer",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (from repeated fluoroscopic exams during tuberculosis treatment, and among bomb survivors)."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page7_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Unlike most radiation-induced cancers, which one follows a threshold dose-response relationship?"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "Leukemia",
              "Skin cancer"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Radiation-induced skin cancer is specifically noted as following a threshold relationship, unlike leukemia and most other radiation-induced malignancies."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Risk Estimates \u2014 Relative, Excess, and Absolute Risk",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Because it's nearly impossible to link any single cancer case to a specific prior radiation exposure, radiobiologists use population-level risk estimates instead:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Type"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ],
                          "attrs": {
                            "header": true
                          }
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Formula"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ],
                          "attrs": {
                            "header": true
                          }
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Meaning"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ],
                          "attrs": {
                            "header": true
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Relative risk",
                                  "marks": [
                                    {
                                      "type": "bold"
                                    }
                                  ]
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Observed cases \u00f7 Expected cases"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "A ratio of 1.0 = no added risk; 1.5 = a 50% higher rate than the unirradiated population"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Excess risk",
                                  "marks": [
                                    {
                                      "type": "bold"
                                    }
                                  ]
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Observed cases \u2212 Expected cases"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "The actual number of \"extra\" cases attributable to radiation"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Absolute risk",
                                  "marks": [
                                    {
                                      "type": "bold"
                                    }
                                  ]
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Slope of the dose-response line"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Cases per population per unit dose; requires at least two known dose levels"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Worked example (relative risk):",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " 227 leukemia cases observed per 100,000 irradiated persons; normal incidence is 150/100,000. Relative risk = 227/150 = "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "1.51",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Radiation and Pregnancy",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The developing embryo/fetus is considered separately because radiosensitivity changes dramatically across gestation. The "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "first trimester is the most radiosensitive period overall",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": ", though the specific type of effect varies by exact timing:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Timing"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ],
                          "attrs": {
                            "header": true
                          }
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Possible effect"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ],
                          "attrs": {
                            "header": true
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "First 2 weeks"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Spontaneous abortion (all-or-none response \u2014 either the pregnancy is lost, or it continues with no observed ill effect)"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "2nd\u201312th weeks (organogenesis)"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Congenital abnormalities, especially CNS; neonatal death if severe enough"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "2nd trimester onward"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Developmental delays (reduced behavioral/cognitive performance)"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Human data (including atomic bomb survivor offspring) show measurable IQ/scholastic performance differences and a small increase in microcephaly, particularly following doses in excess of 100 mGy during organogenesis."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page9_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Exposure during which period of pregnancy carries the greatest overall radiosensitivity concern?"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "First trimester",
              "Third trimester"
            ],
            "answer": 0,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "All observations point to the first trimester \u2014 particularly the period of organogenesis \u2014 as the most radiosensitive window of pregnancy."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Genetic Effects",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Genetic effects are radiobiology's weakest area of human evidence \u2014 essentially all data comes from large-scale animal studies (H.J. Muller's Nobel Prize-winning fruit fly work in 1927; Russell's multi-generation mouse colony studies beginning in 1946). "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "No radiation-induced genetic effects have been observed in atomic bomb survivor descendants",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": ", now studied into the third generation."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Key conclusions from the animal data: radiation-induced mutations are usually harmful, any dose to a germ cell carries some genetic risk, the dose-response relationship is linear and nonthreshold, women are less sensitive than men to genetic effects for most of their reproductive life, and most radiation-induced mutations are recessive (requiring the mutant gene in both parents to be expressed)."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "doubling dose",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " \u2014 the dose that would double the spontaneous mutation rate \u2014 is estimated at roughly 0.5\u20132.5 Gy in humans."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
